वेदि|वेदिः
वेदि f. (-दिः-दी)
	 1. Ground for placing the vessels used at an oblation,
or for binding the victim, or lighting the sacrificial fire; it is
more or less raised and of various shapes; an alter, &c.
	 2. A
quadrangular spot in the court-yard of a temple or palace, usually
furnished with a raised floor or seat, and covered with a roof
supported by pillars: see वितर्द्दि.
	 3. A seal-ring.
	 m. (-दिः) A
Pandit, a teacher.
	 f. (-दिः) A name of SARASWATĪ.
 E. विद् to know,
&c., Unādi aff. इन्, and ङीष् optionally added in the fem. form.
